<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s08e02" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 2: Interval Notation</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 2 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Interpret interval notation.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Convert between inequalities and intervals. 
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 2 Key Terminology</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Interval
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 2 Key Symbols</title>
    <p>
      <md>
        \infty \quad \quad \text{as in}\,\, (5,\infty)
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <definition xml:id="def-interval-notation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <term>Interval notation</term> gives us a different way to represent solutions to inequalities. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    The interval
    <md>
      [3, 5]
    </md>
    refers to all the numbers between 3 and 5, including 3 and including 5. The square brackets communicate that 3 and 5 are included. 
  </p>
  <p>
    The interval [3,5] has the same meaning as the inequality:
    <md>
      3 \le x \le 5
    </md>
  We can represent this interval on a number line by shading in the region between 3 and 5, drawing a solid dot at 3, 
  and drawing a solid dot at 5. The solid dot communicates that 3 and 5 are included.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <caption>The interval [3,5] on a number line.</caption>
    <image width="90%">
      <prefigure label="pf-number-line-1" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(550,100)" margins="5">
      <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-2,5.5,2)">
      <axes axes="horizontal" hticks="(-5,1,5)" arrows="2" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" tick-size="5"/>
      <line endpoints="(3,0),(5,0)" thickness="8" stroke = "red"/>
        <point p="(3,0)" stroke = "red" fill = "red" size="8"/>
          <point p="(5,0)" stroke = "red" fill = "red" size="8"/>
            </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    By contrast, the interval
    <md>
      (3, 5)
    </md>
    refers to all the numbers between 3, not including 3, and not including 5. The parentheses communicate that the 3 and 5 are not included.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interval (3,5) has the same meaning as the inequality
    <md>
      3 \lt x \lt 5
    </md>
    We can represent this interval on a number line by shading in the region between 3 and 5, drawing a hollow dot at 3 and a hollow dot at 5. 
  </p>
  <figure>
    <caption>The interval (3,5) on a number line.</caption>
    <image width="90%">
      <prefigure label="pf-number-line-2" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(550,100)" margins="5">
      <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-2,5.5,2)">
      <axes axes="horizontal" hticks="(-5,1,5)" arrows="2" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" tick-size="5"/>
      <line endpoints="(3,0),(5,0)" thickness="8" stroke = "red"/>
        <point p="(3,0)" stroke = "red" fill = "white" size="8"/>
          <point p="(5,0)" stroke = "red" fill = "white" size="8"/>
            </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Be Careful!</title>
    <p>
      When doing PEMDAS calculations, brackets mean the same thing as parentheses:
      <md>
        3[4-1] \quad \text{ is the same as}\quad 3(4-1)
      </md>
      But when writing intervals, brackets do not mean the same thing as parentheses.
      <md>
        [1,4]\quad \text{ is NOT the same as} \quad (1,4)
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <p>
      Convert the interval (-2,1] to inequality notation and then draw the interval on a number line.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: This interval means all the numbers between -2 and 1. The number -2 is not included because there is a parenthesis next to it. 
      The number 1 is included, because there is a bracket next to it. So, this would correspond to the inequality
      <md>
        -2 \lt x \le 1
      </md>
      On a number line, we shade the region between -2 and 1, with a hollow dot at -2, and a solid dot at 1.
    </p>
    <figure>
    <caption>The interval (-2,1] on a number line.</caption>
    <image width="90%">
      <prefigure label="pf-number-line-3" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(550,100)" margins="5">
      <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-2,5.5,2)">
      <axes axes="horizontal" hticks="(-5,1,5)" arrows="2" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" tick-size="5"/>
      <line endpoints="(-2,0),(1,0)" thickness="8" stroke = "red"/>
        <point p="(-2,0)" stroke = "red" fill = "white" size="8"/>
          <point p="(1,0)" stroke = "red" fill = "red" size="8"/>
            </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Translate the inequality
      <md>
        1 \le x \lt 7
      </md>
      into interval notation
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: The inequality tells us that x is between 1 and 7, including 1, but not including 7. So, this becomes the interval
      <md>
        [1, 7)
      </md>
      with a bracket next to 1 and a parenthesis next to 7.
    </p>
  </example>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Be Careful!</title>
    <p>
      Notice that the variable itself, <m>x</m>, does not appear anywhere in the interval. The interval just shows the smallest and largest values of <m>x</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    Intervals are always written with the smaller value on the left side, to make the order of the numbers on the number line.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Write the inequality in interval notation:
      <md>
        4 \ge x \gt 2
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: This inequality says that <m>x</m> is between 2 and 4, including 4, not including 2. So, we write the interval
      <md>
        (2,4]
      </md>
      
(2,4]
We put the smaller number, 2, on the left side of the interval. We put a parenthesis next to 2 because it is not included. 
We put a bracket next to 4 because it is included.
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    The inequality
    <md>
      x \gt 5
    </md>
    means that <m>x</m> can be anything greater than 5. The interval containing all numbers greater than 5 is written:
    <md>
      (5,\infty)
    </md>
    with the infinity symbol, <m>\infty</m>, on the right side. We use the infinity symbol to communicate that can be <m>x</m> as big as you want.
  </p>
  <p>
    The inequality 
    <md>
      x \lt 7
    </md>
    means that <m>x</m> can be anything less than or equal to 7. The interval containing all numbers less than or equal to 7 is written:
    <md>
      (-7, \infty]
    </md>
    with the negative infinity symbol, <m>-\infty</m>, on the left side. We use the negative infinity symbol to communicate that <m>x</m> can be as small as you want.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Write the inequality <m>x\ge 2</m> in interval notation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Here, <m>x</m> can be anything greater than or equal to 2. So, we would write:
      <md>
        [2, \infty)
      </md>
      We put a bracket next to the 2 to communicate that 2 is included. We use the infinity symbol to communicate that can be <m>x</m> as big as you want.
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Write the inequality <m>x \lt 6</m> in interval notation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Here, <m>x</m> can be anything less than 6. So, we would write:
      <md>
        (-\infty, 6)
      </md>
      We put a parenthesis next to the 6 to communicate that 6 is not included. 
      We use the negative infinity symbol to communicate that <m>x</m> can be as small as you want.
    </p>
  </example>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Be Careful!</title>
    <p>
      Positive <m>\infty</m> will always appear on the right side of the interval because it is bigger than everything.
    </p>
    <p>
      Negative <m>\infty</m> will always appear on the left side of the interval because it is more negative than everything.
    </p>
    <p>
      There will ALWAYS be a parenthesis next to positive or negative <m>\infty</m>, because <m>x</m> can never actually equal infinity.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s08e02">
    <title>Season 8, Episode 2 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Convert the following inequalities to interval notation. Then draw the interval on the number line.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> -4 \leq x \lt 3</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> 0 \lt x \lt 8</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> 2 \lt x \leq 9</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> -6 \lt x \leq 4</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> -7 \lt x \lt 1</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> 3 \leq x</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> x \geq 5</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> x \lt -1</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> 3 \leq x</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> x \leq -2</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> -12 \leq x \lt -5</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> -10 \leq x \leq -3</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> 7 \lt x \leq 15</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    </exercise>

